## **6. The Literary Description (For Use in Manuscript)**\
\
*In the style of your influences:*\
\
**The Traverse existed in the peculiar state of being both impressive engineering achievement and elaborate middle finger to several government departments. Like much of Eloise Shakti's empire, it occupied the space between "technically legal" and "please don't ask too many questions."**\
\
**The journey from Sublime Point to Ruined Castle took two minutes and fifteen seconds by maglev pod, which was precisely two minutes and fifteen seconds longer than most passengers felt comfortable being fired through four kilometers of solid sandstone at speeds that would alarm a responsible engineer. The pods themselves were masterpieces of form following function following paranoia\'97sleek capsules that managed to feel simultaneously like expensive coffins and very fast safety deposit boxes.**\
\
**The tunnel, carved through geology that had been peacefully minding its own business for hundreds of millions of years, featured lighting that someone had optimistically described as "mood setting" and everyone else described as "just bright enough to see your impending doom." At irregular intervals, natural caverns opened like the mountain's attempt at cathedral architecture, reminding passengers that they were traveling through a landscape that predated human anxiety about being underground by a considerable margin.**\
\
**Staff who used the Traverse regularly developed the particular sangfroid of people who'd accepted that their commute involved being fired through the Earth's crust like a particularly well-paid bullet. First-timers arrived at Ruined Castle with the expression of people who'd just been given a practical demonstration of their own mortality. Veterans arrived checking their tablets, having learned that existential dread and email both required management.**\
\
**The existence of the entire system had been hidden from official oversight through a combination of creative accounting, strategic philanthropy, and the useful fact that bureaucrats rarely approved things they didn't understand. Eloise had calculated that asking permission would have taken years and resulted in either denial or a public project. Building first and apologizing never had been cheaper, faster, and infinitely more satisfying.**\
\
